\section{Expose Independence Before Mapping Work}

An IR for parallel compilation must distinguish \emph{permitted} overlap from the compiler's eventual choice of execution resources. Two loop iterations may be independent but too small to justify separate tasks. Conversely, two operations mapped to different devices may still have to execute in order. If the representation exposes only scalar instructions and raw addresses, recovering a regular iteration domain and its memory accesses can be much harder than preserving them in the first place. Wilson and colleagues' \textit{An Overview of the SUIF Compiler System} emphasizes the need to support loop and array dependence analyses alongside conventional scalar optimizations. Stanier and Watson's survey traces how dependence-oriented representations remove ordering that would otherwise conceal available parallelism.

Different IRs expose readiness at different granularities. Ariola and Arvind's \textit{P-TAC: A Parallel Intermediate Language} specifies a parallel reduction model whose intermediate syntax represents the sharing of computations. Its single-assignment I-structures distinguish a not-yet-filled component from a ready one. Adam Randall Smith's \textit{The Parallel Intermediate Language} instead targets data-driven runtimes that use dependencies to decide when tasks can run. In such a runtime, a barrier implemented by having non-preemptable workers spin may prevent the remaining tasks from ever getting a worker. A continuation that depends on completion events instead leaves workers free to run those tasks.

\section{Worked Example: A Two-Stage Stencil}

Suppose a program computes an intermediate array \(T\) from an input \(A\), then produces \(B_i=T_{i-1}+T_i+T_{i+1}\) for interior elements. Assume valid nonvolatile accesses, nonoverlapping arrays, total fixed-width arithmetic, and that no other code reads \(B\) before completion. Partition the index domain into disjoint tiles \(D_j\). Task \(P_j\) produces \(T\) over \(D_j\); task \(Q_j\) writes only the interior output indices in \(D_j\). For contiguous tiles of positive length, a nonempty \(Q_j\) needs \(P_j\) and also the neighboring producers when their tiles contain halo elements it reads. It does not need every \(P_k\) to finish.

The invariant is that a runnable \(Q_j\) has acquired a completion event covering each element it reads and that the associated writes are visible at its execution site. Removing the edge from \(P_{j+1}\) lets \(Q_j\) read an unfilled right-hand halo at its last output index. A test can delay that producer and compare the boundary element with a sequential evaluation. Completion here is stronger than ``the host enqueued the kernel.'' Across devices it may need to include a transfer to the consumer's address space. A shared-host schedule could implement the same dependency with a release by the producer and a corresponding acquire by the consumer.

\section{Mapping the Same Dependencies to Hardware}

Budiu and Goldstein's \textit{Pegasus} uses a representation suitable for predication and speculation in a compiler that translates C into hardware. Here the backend can turn dependence into ready signals, buffering, or scheduled wires rather than software task records. Predication is useful at stencil boundaries: the backend can suppress an out-of-bounds memory access. Executing the access and then masking only its result would not be equivalent if the access can fault or has observable effects. A hardware implementation may additionally constrain throughput by using a limited number of memory ports; those resource constraints are not source-level dependences.

The dossier's Markdown Chapters~25, \textit{Heterogeneous IR}, and 26, \textit{Spatial Compiler}, distinguish placement and communication costs from the computational graph. Keep that distinction in a lowering pipeline: at the array level verify iteration coverage and dependence distances; at the task level verify that every required completion has a producer and a buffer remains live until its last consumer; at the device level verify address spaces, transfer visibility, and any synchronization requirements. Tile size, fusion, and physical placement can be tuned without changing the first two invariants. A trace of a fast schedule is useful for choosing a mapping, but it does not certify that other schedules allowed by the emitted IR will observe the same halo values.
